Mosquito-Borne Alphaviruses (Ross River / Barmah Forest)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Severe symmetrical polyarthralgia, Transient maculopapular rash, Profound prolonged fatigue, History of mosquito exposure in endemic area

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-029
Reason for Visit: Sudden, debilitating joint pain preventing ambulation, accompanied by a rash and extreme fatigue following a recent trip to coastal Western Australia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing an abrupt onset of severe, symmetrical joint stiffness and pain so intense they are unable to stand or perform basic self-care, accompanied by a spreading rash. Urgent physical assessment is required to rule out acute inflammatory polyarthritis, acute rheumatic fever, or severe systemic infection prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up and my knees, ankles, and wrists are completely seized up with pain, and I have zero energy to even move."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced 4 days ago with a low-grade fever, intense generalized myalgia, and prominent muscle stiffness. Over the last 48 hours, the patient developed a sudden onset of severe, debilitating, and symmetrical polyarthralgia primarily affecting the peripheral joints (wrists, knees, ankles). Concurrently, a widespread, transient maculopapular rash has appeared, involving the trunk and limbs. The patient reports a sensation of severe 'pins and needles' and distinct paresthesias in the palms and soles. Crucially, the patient describes profound, severe generalized fatigue and systemic lethargy completely disproportionate to physical exertion. Patient reports a history of recent travel to a known endemic coastal region with high mosquito activity 2 weeks ago.
Functional Status: Bedbound/chairbound. Unable to walk without severe pain and extreme stiffness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to cook or manage basic ADLs due to severe hand and wrist pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, appearing exhausted, moving limbs very deliberately and slowly due to pain.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals severe, symmetrical tenderness and stiffness in the bilateral wrists, MCP joints, knees, and ankles. No gross, hot, erythematous effusions to suggest septic arthritis. Skin exam reveals a diffuse, blanching maculopapular rash over the torso and proximal extremities. Head and neck exam reveals mild, painless, and transient regional lymphadenopathy (cervical). Neurological exam is intact, but subjective paresthesias are noted in the hands and feet. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Severe symmetrical polyarthralgia, Transient maculopapular rash, Profound prolonged fatigue, and History of mosquito exposure in endemic area.
Environmental Observations: Patient unable to open medication bottles or prepare food; arranged for neighbor assistance.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mosquito-Borne Alphavirus (Ross River Virus / Barmah Forest Virus) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Rheumatoid Arthritis Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a prominent transient maculopapular rash and the specific epidemiological history (endemic mosquito exposure), though the joint presentation is highly similar.

Dengue Fever - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of severe retro-orbital pain, high biphasic fever, and the severe thrombocytopenic bleeding risk, though cross-reactivity and symptom overlap occur.

Fifth Disease (Parvovirus B19) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific paresthesias in the palms/soles and the travel history, though serology is required to definitively differentiate the viral arthropathies.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Functional impairment)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severely debilitating symmetrical polyarthropathy and rash following exposure in an endemic mosquito zone. The sudden, severe functional impairment caused by the intense joint pain and profound fatigue necessitated an urgent clinical assessment. A physical examination was crucial to rule out acute, joint-destroying pathologies (like septic arthritis), differentiate from a severe systemic autoimmune crisis, and provide immediate, potent pharmacological symptom relief to restore basic functional mobility. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period would leave the patient immobilized and in severe, untreated pain, fulfilling the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe regular, scheduled NSAIDs (e.g., Naproxen 500mg twice daily with food) for potent anti-inflammatory relief of the polyarthralgia. Add Paracetamol 1g four times daily for synergistic analgesia. Urgent referral for definitive serology: specific viral IgM antibodies or a four-fold rise in IgG titres on acute and convalescent serology, alongside a basic autoimmune screen (RF, CRP) to rule out primary rheumatological disease.
Positive Behavioral Change: By setting alarms to take the anti-inflammatory medication rigidly on a 12-hour schedule rather than waiting for the joint pain to peak, the patient will maintain steady-state pain control; this will reward them with the restored ability to walk to the bathroom and prepare small meals, reinforcing strict adherence to the dosing schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Easy-open medication containers.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7-10 days to review serology results and assess ongoing functional status.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if a single joint becomes suddenly hot, intensely red, and massively swollen (concerning for acute septic arthritis), or if the patient develops an unremitting high fever, severe headache, or severe confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending definitive viral serology; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute rash and fever typically resolve within a week. However, the chronic, fluctuating severe joint pain and extreme fatigue can potentially persist for several months before eventually clearing completely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

While this virus causes severe pain, it does not permanently destroy the joints. Seek immediate emergency care if one specific joint suddenly swells up completely out of proportion to the others, becomes bright red, and feels hot to the touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have contracted a viral infection from a mosquito bite that triggers a massive, temporary inflammatory response specifically targeting the lining of your joints. There is no cure for the virus itself; the treatment relies entirely on aggressively suppressing the inflammation with strong NSAIDs until your immune system clears the infection.
Actionable Steps: Take the NSAID medication strictly with a full meal to prevent stomach ulcers. Rest completely when the fatigue hits; trying to push through the exhaustion will only prolong the recovery phase.

